Chapter: LirSelectorSmoke

 The LIR selector's correctness shapes, packed into one boot for the BVT. The
 self-compile (fixed point) cannot see any of these: the compiler either does
 not contain the shape, or self-compiles the same whether the shape is right or
 wrong. Each function here is a miscompile that shipped or would have shipped,
 pinned by its ANSWER -- a wrong codegen decision changes the number, not the
 structure, so only a `.expected` catches it.

  cites Foreword chapter Console

Section: Defs

 A higher-order function whose parameter name shadows a same-arity global. The
 body must call the PARAMETER (indirect, through the closure), not the global
 the name shadows. s-apply s-add 3 4 is 3 + 4 = 7; a call-root resolver that
 ignores the local binding calls the global s-sub and answers -1. The selector
 declines this to the tree, which resolves locals first.

  s-sub : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s-sub (a) (b) = a - b

  s-add : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s-add (a) (b) = a + b

  s-apply : (Integer, Integer -> Integer), Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s-apply (s-sub) (x) (y) = s-sub x y

 A join feeding a second branch in one arm, non-tail (the + 7 forces it).
 Coalescing must not hoist the inner join's definition onto a path where the
 outer join vreg is otherwise defined -- that double-def is a legal overwrite
 the emitter handles but the structural verifier rejects (CDX9007). Refusing
 the fold keeps the program compilable. s-join 0 = 6, s-join 5 = 7.

  s-join : Integer -> Integer
  s-join (n) = (let v = if n > 0 then n + 1 else n - 1 in if n == 0 then v else 0) + 7

 Tail recursion whose tail call passes a compound accumulator argument that
 reads the loop parameter the first argument decrements. The accumulator must
 read the OLD parameter: s-tail 1 7 is 7 - 1 + 2 = 8. Writing the decremented
 value first makes the accumulator read it (7 - 0 + 2 = 9). The selector orders
 the back-edge writes topologically; the tree emitter does not for this shape.

  s-tail : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s-tail (n) (acc) = if n <= 0 then acc else s-tail (n - 1) (acc - n + 2)

 Five parameters and a division, so the allocator runs out of argument
 registers and masks RDX -- a parameter cannot keep its arrival register and
 the entry moves must be a parallel move. Emitted in order they clobber: this
 is the col-hue shape that reported red as yellow. s-clash 9 0 0 9 3 = 0.

  s-clash : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s-clash (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) =
    if d == a then (b - c) * 6 / e
    else if d == b then (c - a) * 6 / e + 120
    else (a - b) * 6 / e + 240

 The function result is a use. s is both the result and the source of the one
 join move; the else arm's division keeps the dead let alive through
 simplification. A coalescer that counts only instruction operands folds the
 add into the join vreg and the result reads an undefined vreg (RAX by
 default). s-result 5 b = 5 + 1 = 6 for every b.

  s-result : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s-result (a) (b) = let s = a + 1 in let y = (if b > 0 then s else 100 / (b * b + 1)) in s

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (show (s-apply s-add 3 4))
    print-line-uni (show (s-join 0))
    print-line-uni (show (s-join 5))
    print-line-uni (show (s-join (-2)))
    print-line-uni (show (s-tail 5 (-2)))
    print-line-uni (show (s-tail 1 7))
    print-line-uni (show (s-clash 9 0 0 9 3))
    print-line-uni (show (s-clash 0 9 0 9 3))
    print-line-uni (show (s-clash 0 0 9 9 3))
    print-line-uni (show (s-result 5 (-2)))
    print-line-uni (show (s-result 5 3))
  end
